# Words that measure a fraction between "a" or a determiner and the ordinal:
# "a full third of the month".  After "the" they leave a day: "the whole fifth
# of may" is the whole of that day.
# "third" as a fraction: Wiktionary, "third", noun sense 2, "One of three
# equal parts of a whole."  Each word's measuring sense, from
# Wiktionary unless named (adjective sense number, then its definition):
#   full      3    "Total, entire."
#   whole     1    "Entire, undivided."
#   entire    1    "Whole; complete."
#   solid     18   "(of time) Entire, complete."
#   fair      Dictionary.com, adjective 3: "moderately large; ample."
#   clear     17   "Without diminution; in full; net."
#   complete  1    "With all parts included; with nothing missing; full."
#   large     1    "Of considerable or relatively great size or extent."
#   mere      1    "Just, only; no more than"
#   extra     1    "Beyond what is due, usual, expected, or necessary; ...
#                  additional"
#   total     1    "Entire; relating to the whole of something."
#   good      7.3  "Full; entire; at least as much as."
full
whole
entire
solid
fair
clear
complete
large
mere
extra
total
good
